Page 1112 angkong: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|nan-hbl|俺公|tr=án-kong|t=paternal grandfather}}. Compare {{cog|id|engkong}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|b=+}} {{tlb|tl|Chinese Filipino}}

# [[paternal]] [[grandfather]]; [[paternal]] [[grandpa]]
# {{n-g|[[term of address]] for one's paternal grandfather}}
#: {{syn|tl|lolo|ingkong}}
#: {{coord|tl|ama}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2006|author=Christine S. Bellen|editor=Carla M. Pacis|publisher=UP Press|title=Bagets: an anthology of Filipino young adult fiction|chapter=Trese|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?id=7d1FOdaBfhQC&pg=PA12&dq=angkong&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiR9eu0xLn7AhUSbd4KHfK-Dj8Q6AF6BAgDEAI#v=onepage&q=ama&f=false|page=11|text=Mestisang Tsina naman si Nanay. Negosyante sina Ama at '''Angkong''' ko. Purong Tsino si Angkong. Lumikas mula sa Macao ang pamilya nila at dito sa Pilipinas nagtayo ng isang maliit na tindahan hanggang sa lumago ito at naging isang grocery.|t=Mom is a Chinese mestiza. My grandmother and '''grandfather''' are businesspeople. Grandpa is a pure Chinese. Their family evacuated from Macau and it was here in the Philippines where they started a small store until it flourished and became a grocery.}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2006|author=Christine S. Bellen|editor=Carla M. Pacis|publisher=UP Press|title=Bagets: an anthology of Filipino young adult fiction|chapter=Trese|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?id=7d1FOdaBfhQC&pg=PA12&dq=angkong&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiR9eu0xLn7AhUSbd4KHfK-Dj8Q6AF6BAgDEAI#v=onepage&q=ama&f=false|page=11|text=Sa Pilipinas na napangasawa ni '''Angkong''' si Ama. Pilipina ang nanay ni Ama pero sila ang mas mahigpit sa mga pamahiing Tsino.|t=It was in the Philippines already where '''Grandpa''' married Grandma. Grandma's mother is a Filipina but they are the ones who are stricter in Chinese superstitions.}}

====See also====
* {{l|tl|ingkong}}

