Page 1480 -asyon: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|-siyon|-syon||following consonants}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|es|-ación}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|-asyón}}

===Suffix===
{{tl-noun|-asyón|b=+}}

# {{n-g|Used in forming a noun to represent a property corresponding to a verb, usually Spanish-derived. Also used to replace English {{m|en|-ation}} in words adapted directly to Tagalog upon borrowing:}} [[-ation]]
#: {{syn|tl|pag-}}

====Usage notes====
* Almost all Tagalog words with the ending are borrowed from Spanish, with some being pseudo-Hispanic adaptations of English borrowings ending with {{m|en|-ation}} to match existing Spanish loanwords with that suffix (e.g. {{m|tl|populasyón}}, from {{m|en|population}}; {{m|tl|polusyón}} from {{m|en|pollution}}). Compare the usage of Indonesian {{m|id|-asi}} (from Dutch {{m|nl|-atie}}) to replace {{m|en|-ation}} in some English borrowings in a similar vein to harmonize with existing Dutch borrowings ending with that suffix.
* Rarely used to form authentic words (compared to the synonymous native equivalent {{m|tl|pag-}}), except for surface analysis of existing words (e.g. {{m|tl|modernisasyon}}), which can be analyzed as {{m|tl|modernisa}} + ''-asyon'', or as replacement of English [[-ation]] as explained above.
* Its use in adapting [[-ation]] in English loanwords has been proscribed by some linguists, most notably {{w|Virgilio S. Almario}} during his tenure in the Commission of the Philippine Language, as an example of ''{{w|Siyokoy (linguistics)|siyokoy}}'', preferring using an equivalent with the prefix [[pag-]] or borrowing and respelling the equivalent Spanish of the English.

====Derived terms====
* {{suffixsee|tl}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:Pambansang Diksiyonaryo}}
-------- end text --------
