Page 2841 Bisaya: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|ceb|Bisaya}}, of uncertain origin, possibly {{der|tl|sa|-}}. Possibly related to {{cog|sa|विषय|t=kingdom; dominion; region; territory; domain; realm}}, according to Potet (2016).<ref>{{R:tl:Potet|TBC|pages=278}}</ref> Compare {{ncog|km|វិស័យ}}, {{ncog|th|วิสัย}}, {{ncog|pi|visaya}}, {{cog|bsy|Bisaya}}, {{cog|bsb|Bisaya}}, {{cog|txx|Bisaya}}. Other theories include:

* From {{cog|ms-old|Sri Vijaya||{{w|Srivijaya|Sri Vijaya}}}}, from {{cog|sa|[[श्री]] [[विजय]]|tr=śrī vijayá}}. During the 1950s to the 1960s, various authors have claimed that ''Bisaya'' is derived from Sri Vijaya, claiming the early settlers of Panay were either from Sri Vijaya or subjects of Sri Vijaya. However, linguist Eugene Verstraelen refutes these claims by pointing out that Vijaya would have been "''Bidaya''" or "''Biraya''". Compare {{cog|ms|bisai}}, from {{cog|ta|விசை}}.
* From {{cog|sa|वैश्य|tr=vaíśya}} which would have been what Indian traders and/or pirates might have described the settlers of Panay.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|Bisayà}}

===Adjective===
{{tl-adj|Bisayà|b=+}}

# [[Visayan]] {{gl|pertaining to Visayas}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|Bisayà|b=+}}

# [[Visayan]] {{gl|person from the Visayan region}}
# [[Visayan]] {{gl|dialect continuum}}
# [[Cebuano]] {{gl|speaker of Cebuano}}
#: {{syn|tl|Cebuano}}
# {{lb|tl|strictly}} [[Cebuano]] [[language]]
#: {{syn|tl|Cebuano}}

====Usage notes====
In the noun senses, {{w|Tagalog language|Tagalog}} speakers most commonly use this term to denote an ethnic {{w|Cebuano people|Cebuano}}, a {{w|Cebuano language|Cebuano}} speaker, and the {{w|Cebuano language}}. {{w|Visayans}} (in the broader sense of natives of the {{w|Visayas}}), beside {{w|Cebuano people|Cebuanos}}, such as {{w|Hiligaynon people|Hiligaynon}} and {{w|Waray people|Waray}}, also refer to their respective native languages as "Bisaya", but their respective speakers often use the demonym "Bisaya" to mean {{w|Cebuano people|Cebuano}} instead of {{w|Visayans}} in general, thus, using "Bisaya" in that broader sense (Cebuanos aside) is usually confusing and/or offensive.

{{w|Cebuano language|Cebuano}} speakers outside of {{w|Cebu}} province generally use "Bisaya" for the {{w|Cebuano language|Cebuano}} varieties in {{w|Cebu}}, and the local varieties of {{w|Cebuano language|Cebuano}} usually have their own names (e.g. Boholano, Kana).

In {{w|Mindanao}}, there are also ethnic groups like the {{w|Surigaonon people|Surigaonon}} and {{w|Butuanon people|Butuanon}} who speak a {{w|Bisayan languages|Visayan language}} besides {{w|Cebuano language|Cebuano}}, {{w|Hiligaynon language|Hiligaynon}} or {{w|Waray language|Waray}}, but they would use "Bisaya" to refer to a {{w|Cebuano language|Cebuano}} or {{w|Hiligaynon language|Hiligaynon}} speaker (and their respective languages as well); calling them or their native language "Bisaya" instead of their respective local name is confusing and/or offensive. Nevertheless, "Bisaya" is the preferred ethnonym for native {{w|Cebuano language|Cebuano}} speakers in {{w|Mindanao}} who do not necessarily descend from the {{w|Cebuano people|Cebuanos}} of {{w|Cebu}}, and the {{w|Cebuano language}}, in turn, is called ''Binisaya'', as a result of migrant ethnic groups from [[Luzon]] and indigenous Mindanaoans assimilation into the majority Cebuano-speaking society over the years because Mindanao is a melting pot of cultures due to the migrants from Luzon and Visayas moving to their new homelands in Mindanao seeking economic opportunities mainly in agriculture since the early 20th century.

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|Kabisayaan|magbisaya|Binisaya|bisayain|binisaya|ipabisaya|mabisaya|makabisaya|makibisaya|makipagbisaya|mangbisaya|mapagbisaya|napakabisaya|pabisaya|pagbisaya|pagkabisaya|pagkakabisaya|pakibisaya|pakikipagbisaya|palabisaya|pangbisaya|pinakabisaya|tagabisaya|tagapagbisaya|magbisayaan|bisayang-bisaya}}

====See also====
{{col3|tl|Pintados}}

