Page 3506 buwisit: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|bwisit}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|nan-hbl|無衣食|tr=bô-ui-si̍t|t=to be [[out of luck]]; to have [[bad luck]]|lit=without clothes and food}}. Compare {{cog|ceb|buysit}} and {{cog|tsg|bulsit}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|+<a^annoying; unlucky><n^annoyance; misfortune><int^damn, hell>|buwisít<a^annoyed>|new=1}}

===Adjective===
{{tl-adj|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|vulgar|colloquial|offensive}} [[annoying]]; [[vexing]]
#: {{syn|tl|nakakainis|nakakayamot|nakakasuya|nakakagalit}}
# [[unlucky]]; [[jinx]]; [[ill]]-[[omened]]
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2000|author=Joey A. Arrogante|title=Pagbasa at Pagsulat: Pangkolekiyo|publisher=Rex Bookstore, Inc.|isbn=9789712328480|page=147
|passage=Marami na ang naliligaw at nadidisgrasya dito . Tuloy, sinasabing ang balite ay '''buwisit''' na puno . Ngunit hindi ito pinuputol dahil masama raw sa halip ito'y sinusunog para umalis ang mga maligno .
|t=There are already a lot of people who are lost or seriously injured here. Therefore, the ''balite'' is said to be an '''unlucky''' tree. But cutting it down is bad and it is rather burned for the evil spirits to leave.}}

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|buwisitin|kabuwisitan|ikabuwisit|makabuwisit|mabuwisit|pambuwisit|mambuwisit|nakabubuwisit}}

