Page 3839 d: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From {{bor|tl|es|d}}. Each pronunciation has a different source:
* Filipino alphabet pronunciation is influenced by {{der|tl|en|d}}.
* Abecedario pronunciation is from {{der|tl|es|d}}.
* Abakada alphabet pronunciation is influenced by Baybayin character {{m|tl|{{tl-bay sc|da}}}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-IPA|di|pre={{sense|letter name|Filipino alphabet}}}}
{{tl-IPA|de|pre={{sense|letter name|Abecedario}}}}
{{tl-IPA|da|pre={{sense|letter name|Abakada alphabet}}}}
{{tl-IPA|pre={{sense|phoneme}}}}
* {{rhymes|tl|i|e|a|s=1}}

====Letter====
{{head|tl|letters|lower case||upper case|D|Baybayin spelling|{{tl-bay sc|di}}|f3sc=Tglg|cat2=terms with Baybayin script}}

# {{Latn-def-lite|tl|letter|4|di|alphabet name={{w|Filipino alphabet}}}}
# {{lb|tl|historical}} {{Latn-def-lite|tl|letter|5|de|alphabet name={{w|Filipino orthography#Adoption of the Latin script|Abecedario}}}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Sometimes switched with the letter {{angbr|{{l|tl|r}}}} between vowels, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}} in a word due to lenition of {{IPAchar|/d/}} to {{IPAchar|/ɾ/}} such as {{m|tl|dadaan}} can become {{m|tl|daraan}}. Compare [[flapping]] in pronunciation of {{ncog|en|medal}}. The letter does not change if the next consonant is an {{IPAchar|/ɾ/}} (such as {{m|tl|madurog}} does not become {{m|tl|marurog}}) or {{IPAchar|/l/}} (such as {{m|tl|madilim}} does not become {{m|tl|marilim}}). 
* Some words starting with the letter can also become {{angbr|{{l|tl|r}}}} if the last letter of the preceding word is a vowel, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}}. Examples are {{m|tl|daw}}/{{m|tl|raw}}, {{m|tl|dito}}/{{m|tl|rito}}, {{m|tl|dami}}/{{m|tl|rami}}, and {{m|tl|damot}}/{{m|tl|ramot}}.
* On all cases stated above, it is acceptable whether {{angbr|d}} or {{angbr|r}} is used. However, the said phenomena do not occur on proper nouns nor recent loan words. 
* In the {{w|Tagalog language#Dialects|Teresa-Morong}} dialect, the letter {{m|tl|r}} may interchange with the letter on any position in the word even when not followed by a vowel, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}}. (ex. {{m|tl|ragat}}/{{m|tl|dagat}} and {{m|tl|bayar}}/{{m|tl|bayad}}). Exceptions are recent loanwords, or if the next consonant after a {{IPAchar|/d/}} is {{IPAchar|/ɾ/}} (such as in {{m|tl|durog}}) or {{IPAchar|/l/}} (such as in {{m|tl|dila}}). 
* Often switched with the letter {{angbr|r}} on non-initial positions in early texts which may indicate ancient pronunciation of words.

=====See also=====
* {{list:Latin script letters/tl}}

