===References===
<references>
<ref name="Pambansang Diksiyonaryo">{{R:Pambansang Diksiyonaryo}}</ref>
<ref name="Guillermo">{{cite-book|last=Guillermo|first=Artemio R.|title=Historical Dictionary of the Philippines|edition=Third|year=2012|pageurl=https://www.google.com.ph/books/edition/Historical_Dictionary_of_the_Philippines/wmgX9M_yETIC?hl=en&gbpv=1&printsec=frontcover&dq=diwata&pg=140|page=140|publisher=Scarecrow Press|text=In ancient Filipino culture, the diwata or anito was the dominant concept in the religion. The anito concept was pervasive in Luzon, while the diwata prevailed in the Visayas and Mindanao.}}</ref>
<ref name="Hislop">{{cite-journal|author=Hislop, Stephen K.|title=Anitism: A Survey of Religious Beliefs Native to the Philippines|work=Asian Studies|volume=9|issue=2|year=1971|url=http://www.asj.upd.edu.ph/mediabox/archive/ASJ-09-02-1971/hislop-anitism-survey-religious%20beliefs-native-philippines.pdf|page=146|passage=With the Bisayans, the word is the equivalent
of Tagalog anito.|accessdate=14 September 2020|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180707172324/http://www.asj.upd.edu.ph/mediabox/archive/ASJ-09-02-1971/hislop-anitism-survey-religious%20beliefs-native-philippines.pdf|archivedate=7 July 2018}}</ref>
<ref name="Potet">{{R:tl:Potet|ABCT|page=268|quote=This term was borrowed from Visayan in modern times. It is not entered in old Tagalog dictionaies. Its interpretation as a Spanish feminine one because of its final {{IPAchar|/a/}} is typical of 20th-century misunderstandings.}}</ref>
<ref name="Natural History">{{cite-journal|author=Kroeber, A.L. |work=Anthropological Papers of the American Museum of Natural History|volume=XIX|year=1918|publisher=American Museum of Natural History|number=II|title=The History of Philippine Civilization as Reflected in Religious Nomenclature|passage=Diwata, devata, dewatu, dewa, etc., gods or spirits generically.  Bisaya, Subanun, Mandaya, Bagobo, Tirurai, Magindanao, Manobo, Tagbanua, Batak.}}</ref>
<ref name="N&S">{{R:Vocabulario de la lengua tagala 1860}}</ref>
<ref name="DLS">{{R:Vocabulario de la lengua tagala}}</ref>
<ref name="SB 1613">{{R:Vocabulario de la lengua tagala 1613|44|Alejarſe) Divata [(pp)] aparte remote}}</ref>
