Page 4673 diyan: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|dian||now|dialectal}}
* {{alt|tl|d'yan||contraction}}
* {{alt|tl|diyaan|dyan|dyaan||nonstandard}}
* {{alt|tl|jan||Internet slang|text messaging}}
* {{alt|tl|riyan||dialectal|Rizal|after words ending with vowels, ''{{angbr|w}}'', or ''{{angbr|y}}'' (in standard Tagalog)}}

===Etymology===
The latter half of the word is possibly {{m|tl|iyan}}. See also {{m|tl|dito}}, {{m|tl|doon}}, {{m|tl|niyan}}, {{m|tl|nito}}, {{m|tl|niyon}}/{{m|tl|noon}}, and other Tagalog demonstrative pronouns. Compare {{cog|bcl|diyan}}, {{cog|pag|ditan}}, and {{cog|mbb|diyan}}. Meanwhile, the former half is possibly related to {{cog|ms|di}} and {{cog|id|di}} as a likely cognate.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|diyán|IPA2=di7án|a2=dialectal|IPA3=+|a3=dialectal}}

===Adverb===
{{tl-adv|diyán|b=+}}

# [[there]] {{gl|far from the speaker, but near the person addressed}}

====Usage notes====
* When the preceding word ends with a vowel, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}}, {{m|tl|riyan}} is used instead, but is not often used in conversation. Other words with this phenomenon include {{m|tl|dito}}, {{m|tl|doon}}, {{m|tl|daw}}, and {{m|tl|din}}.

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|naririyan|nariyan|pumariyan|nandiyan|pumariyan|magdiyan|dumiyan|tagariyan}}

====See also====
{{tl-dem pronouns}}

-------- end text --------
