Page 4726 doon: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|roon}}
* {{alt|tl|do'n}}
* {{alt|tl|ro'n}}

===Etymology===
The latter half of the word is possibly {{m|tl|iyon}} or {{m|tl|yaon}}. See also {{m|tl|niyon}}/{{m|tl|noon}}, {{m|tl|ganoon}}/{{m|tl|gayon}}, {{m|tl|dito}}, {{m|tl|diyan}}, {{m|tl|niyan}}, {{m|tl|nito}}, and other Tagalog demonstrative pronouns. Meanwhile, the former half is possibly related to {{cog|ms|di}} and {{cog|id|di}} as a likely cognate.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-IPA|do7ón}}
{{tl-IPA|don}} {{q|colloquial}}
{{tl-IPA|dun}} {{q|colloquial}}
* {{hyph|tl|do|on}}

===Adverb===
{{tl-adv|doón|b=+}}

# [[yonder]]; [[there]] {{gl|far from both the speaker and the person addressed}}

====Usage notes====
* When the preceding word ends with a vowel, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}}, {{m|tl|roon}} is used instead, but the distinction isn't always made. Other words with this phenomenon include {{m|tl|dito}}, {{m|tl|diyan}}, {{m|tl|daw}}, and {{m|tl|din}}.

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|dumoon|naroroon|naroon|paroo't-parito|pumaroon|paroroon|bukad sa roon|kinaroroonan|magkaroon|pagkakaroon|pagparoon|paroonan|nandoon|tagaroon|mayroon}}

