Page 7326 isain: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Compare {{m|tl|isa}} and {{m|tl|-in}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-IPA|isa7in}}
* {{hyph|tl|i|sa|in}}

===Numeral===
{{tl-num|b=+}} {{tlb|tl|obsolete}}

# [[one]]
#: {{syn|tl|isa}}

====Usage notes====
* Fr. Noceda & Fr. Sanlucar (1860) mentioned a series of the first ten numerals, which they said to be used in the ancient times. Numbers in the series from 1-10 were: {{m-self|tl|isain}}, {{m-self|tl|duwain}}, {{m-self|tl|mampat}}, {{m-self|tl|agyo}}, {{m-self|tl|tundong}}, {{m-self|tl|kala}}, {{m-self|tl|manapit}},  {{m-self|tl|saga}}, {{m-self|tl|bulaid}}, and {{m-self|tl|turo}}. However, according to Blake (1907), the series was hardly possible to be more ancient because the series does not follow the common property of the Malayo-Polynesian family. Furthermore, Brandsetter (1902), thought that Fr. Noceda & Fr. Sanlucar misunderstood, and they represented a series of secret numbers or the numerals of another language.

===Further reading===
* {{R:Vocabulario de la lengua tagala 1860}}
* {{cite-journal|first=Frank R.|last=Blake|title=Contributions to Comparative Philippine Grammar, II|work=Journal of the American Oriental Society|page=199–253|year=1907|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/592772|volume=28|issn=0003-0279|doi=10.2307/592772}}
* {{cite-book|first=Renward|last=Brandstetter|title=Tagalen und Madagassen: eine sprachvergleichende Darstellung als Orientierung für Ethnographen und Sprachforscher|year=1902|publisher=Geschw. Doleschal, Nachfolger J. Eisenring|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?redir_esc=y&id=zIIxAQAAMAAJ&q=isain#v=onepage&q&f=false|page=10}}
* {{R:tl:Potet|NUOT|page=22}}

{{C|tl|One}}
-------- end text --------
