Page 7571 jowa: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|diyowa|dyowa}}

===Etymology===
Alteration of {{m|tl|asawa}} using gay slang prefix {{m|tl|jo-}}.<ref>{{R:Tagalog Slang Dictionary|67}}</ref> Also, possibly a {{glossary|metathesis}} of the first syllables of the words in {{m|tl|wala|walang}} {{m|es|jugar|joga}}, with the second element from {{ncog|es|jugar}}, {{lit|no play|nocap=1}}, implying a serious relationship.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|jowà,+|new=1}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|jowà|head2=+|b=+}} {{tlb|tl|slang|originally|gay slang}}

# [[lover]]; [[boyfriend]] or [[girlfriend]]
#: {{syn|tl|kasintahan|nobyo|nobya|q2=male|q3=female|karelasyon|katipan|kasuyo|siyota|q7=slang|bata|q8=colloquial}}
#* '''2015''', Gretisbored, ''THE JILTED BRIDE'', Margaret S. Sanapo
#*: {{quote|tl|Ganyan lang iyan dahil may pinagdadaanan,&quot; anas ko din sa bakla. "Baka  nagdududa sa ating dalawa, hindi kaya? Baka iniisip na jowa mo ako. Yuck, kadiri! Hinding-hindi kita papatulan" &quot;As if naman papatulan kita. Bwisit kang  bakla ka!}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2002|author=Corazon D. Villareal; Lily Rose R. Tope; Patricia May B. Jurilla|title=Ruptures & Departures|isbn=9789719300212
|passage=Sobrang majubis naman ang '''jowa''' mo, sister. May unan ka na, may kama ka pa! In the name of fun.}}
# [[spouse]]; [[husband]] or [[wife]]
#: {{syn|tl|asawa}}

