Page 8789 kikyam: -------- begin text --------
[[File:2581Foods_Fruits_Vegetables_Cuisine_Bulacan_08.jpg |thumb|''Kikiam Streetfood'']]
[[File:Ngoh Hiang (52705597159).jpg |thumb|''Kikiam Dish Topping'']]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|kikiam|kekyam}}
* {{alt|tl|que-quian}}
* {{alt|tl|kikiyam|kuwekyam||dated}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|nan-hbl|-}}, the first syllable of which most likely is from {{m|nan-hbl|雞|tr=ke|t=chicken}} (as noted by Manuel (1948), though the chief ingredient of the dish nowadays is pork). The second syllable is unclear, though it may possibly be from:
* From {{m|nan-hbl|繭|tr=kián|t=cocoon}}. Compare {{w|Taiwanese Hokkien|Taiwanese}} {{noncog|nan-hbl|肉繭|tr=bah-kián|t=ngo hiang}}.
* From {{m|nan-hbl|鹹|tr=kiâm|t=viand; rice topping}}. See also {{m|tl|kiyampong}}, {{m|tl|tawkiam}}.
* From {{m|nan-hbl|卷|tr=kńg|t=roll}}. Compare {{m|nan-hbl|雞卷|tr=koe-kńg|t=ngo hiang}}, though unlikely since dialects that use the pronunciation "''ke''" for {{m|nan-hbl|雞}} would use "''kúiⁿ''" for {{m|nan-hbl|卷}}.

The slang sense is from the first syllable being homophonous to the last syllable of {{m|tl|puki}} according to Zorc (1993).

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|+,kikyám|new=1}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|+|head2=kikyám|b=+}}

# {{w|kikiam}} {{gl|Philippine variant of {{w|ngo hiang}}}}
# {{lb|tl|slang}} [[vagina]]
#: {{syn|tl|puke|kiki}}

====See also====
{{col3|tl|kiyampong|agridulse}}

===References===
* {{R:Pambansang Diksiyonaryo}}
* {{R:CEDOF|2|106}}
* {{R:tl:Manuel 1948|e=Kᵘwekyam|page=34}}
* {{R:Tagalog Slang Dictionary|117}}
* {{cite-book|last=Barclay|first=Thomas|entry=*kién - koe-kién|title=Supplement to Dictionary of the Vernacular or Spoken Language of Amoy|entryurl=https://archive.org/details/chineseenglish00doug/page/104/mode/2up?q=%22koe-kién%22|year=1923|page=104|location=Tainan|publisher=Presbyterian Church of England}}
* {{cite-book|last=Barclay|first=Thomas|entry=koe - koe-kién|title=Supplement to Dictionary of the Vernacular or Spoken Language of Amoy|entryurl=https://archive.org/details/chineseenglish00doug/page/116/mode/2up?q=%22koe-kién%22|year=1923|page=116|location=Tainan|publisher=Presbyterian Church of England}}

