Page 10389 Linggo: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|Ling.||abbreviation}}
* {{alt|tl|Lingo||proscribed|archaic|Abecedario orthography}}
* {{alt|tl|Dingo||obsolete|Abecedario orthography}}
* {{alt|tl|Dinggo||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
Zorc (1985) surmises it to possibly be from {{bor|tl|es|domingo||Sunday}}, which was possibly mistakenly analyzed as {{m|tl|luminggo}} (e.g. ''Luminggo na'', "It's Sunday", which could have been taken to mean "It's been a week"), from which the word is derived by removing the seeming infix {{m|tl|-um-}} and early change from {{IPAchar|/d/}} to {{IPAchar|/l/}}. However, according to Wolff (1976), it could also be from {{bor|tl|ms|minggu||week}}, which is from {{der|tl|pt|domingo||Sunday}}. Wolff argues that the change in initial nasal to {{IPAchar|/l/}} is also attested for {{w|Tagalog language|Tagalog}} {{m|tl|langka}} and {{w|Malay language|Malay}} {{m|ms|nangka}}. Ultimately from {{der|tl|LL.|diēs Dominicus|t=Sunday|lit=day of the Lord}}. {{doublet|tl|Dominggo}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-IPA|Linggó}}
* {{hyph|tl|Ling|go}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|Linggó|b=+}}

# [[Sunday]]
#: {{syn|tl|Dominggo|q1=archaic}}

====Derived terms====
