===Etymology===
From {{compound|tl|may|sayad}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-IPA}}
* {{hyph|tl|may sa|yad}}

===Adjective===
{{tl-adj|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|offensive}} [[retarded]]; having [[mental retardation]]
#: {{syn|tl|may pahid|may saltik|baliw|abno|ulaga|usbaw|tungaw|mongoloid}}
#* {{quote-text|tl|year=2003|title=Ani: Literary Journal of the Cultural Center of the Philippines
|passage=&quot;'''May sayad''' yata sa utak &#39;yung babae, nakuha pa &#39;kong ngitian,&quot; medyo kalmado na nitong salita. Muli akong tumingin sa labas ng bintana. Nakangiting pinagmasdan ko ang labas.
|translation="That woman's apparently a retard, [because] she even knew to smile at me", he said rather calmly. I looked again outside the window. I'm smiling observing what's outside.}}
#* {{quote-text|tl|year=2008|author=Josel Nicolas|title=Palalim nang palalim, padilim nang padilim at iba pang kuwento ng lagim
|passage=Sari-saring tsismis ang kumakalat tungkol kay Aling Valen. Kesyo iniwan daw ito ng asawa at ipinagpalit sa ibang babae o di kaya&#39;y '''may sayad''' daw ito sa utak, at  kung anu-ano pa. Pero wala kang makukuha ni anumang reaksiyon mula rito.
|translation=There are numerous rumors about Mother Valen. They even said her wife left her and exchanged her for another woman, if not, they say she is mentally retarded, and a lot more. But you can't get any reactions from here.}}

====Usage notes====
* As persons with mental disorders are socially stigmatized in the Philippines, this is considered offensive.
-------- end text --------
