Page 13647 -ng: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-IPA}}

===Etymology 1===

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|tl|-g||for roots ending in ([[n]])}}
* {{alt|tl|-ng-||for [[compound]]s with preceding [[stem]] ending in a [[vowel]].}}
* {{alt|tl|-m-||for [[compound]]s with initial [[stem]] ending in [[vowel]]s, but the next [[stem]] starts with ([[b]]) or ([[p]]).}}
* {{alt|tl|-n-||for [[compound]]s with preceding [[stem]] ending in a [[vowel]], but next [[stem]] starts with the letter ([[d]]), ([[s]]), or ([[t]]).}}
* {{alt|tl|-g-||for [[compound]]s with preceding [[stem]] ending with letter ([[n]]).}}

====Ligature====
<!-- "-ng", "-g", and "na" (those three exclusively) is a separate part of speech entirely, called "pang-angkop" (ligature). -->
{{tl-head|ligature|b=+|nosuffix=1}}

# {{n-g|[[enclitic|Enclitic]] [[suffix]], connecting [[adjective]]s to [[noun]]s and vice versa, after words ending in [[vowel]]s, except [[compound word]]s where the next [[stem]] starts with the letter ([[b]]) or ([[p]]).}}
#: {{syn|tl|na|-g|-ng-|-m-|-n-|-g-}}
#: {{ux|tl|Maganda'''ng''' bulaklak|Pretty flower}}
# {{n-g|[[enclitic|Enclitic]] [[suffix]], connecting [[adverb]]s to [[verb]]s and vice versa, after words ending in [[vowel]]s, except [[compound word]]s where the next [[stem]] starts with the letter ([[b]]) or ([[p]]).}}
#: {{syn|tl|na|-g}}
#: {{ux|tl|Bigla siya'''ng''' nagsalita.|S/he suddenly spoke.}}

===Etymology 2===
Possibly from {{der|tl|es|-ín}}. See {{m|tl|-ing}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|tl|-ing||for roots ending in consonants}}

====Suffix====
{{tl-proper noun|b=+}}

# {{n-g|[[diminutive|Diminutive]] [[suffix]], used to form [[diminutive]]s of [[given name]]s ending in [[vowel]]s, often one already shortened or with a diminutive suffix.}}
#: {{uxi|tl|Pepe'''ng'''|little Joseph}}
#: {{uxi|tl|Is'''ang'''|little Isabel}}

===Derived terms===
* {{suffixsee|tl}}

===See also===
* {{l|tl|-ing}}
* {{l|tl|-ito}}
* {{l|tl|-ita}}
* {{l|tl|-oy}}
* {{l|tl|-ay}}

{{cln|tl|enclitics|diminutive suffixes}}

-------- end text --------
Page 13648 ng: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From earlier {{m|tl|nang}}, from Proto-Greater Central Philippine ''*naN'' (indefinite object marker). See also {{m|tl|ni}} and {{m|tl|ang}}.

The two-letter spelling originates in a now defunct Spanish language abbreviation convention of drawing a tilde over the abbreviated word, in this case {{m|tl|nang}}, later rendered variously as {{m|tl|ng̃}}, {{m|tl|ñg}} or {{m|tl|n͠g}}. The complications of typing {{angbr|ñ}}, {{angbr|g̃}} or any such combination with tilde using English language typewriters in the American occupation period of the Philippines, caused it to be dropped in writing. Compare {{m|tl|mga}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-pr}}

====Preposition====
{{tl-prep|b=+}}

# ({{n-g|indirect or object marker for nouns or phrases other than personal names}})
#: {{ux|tl|K'''in'''ain '''ng pusa''' ang isda.|'''The/a cat''' ate the fish.}}
#: {{ux|tl|K'''um'''ain '''ng isda''' ang pusa.|The cat ate '''the/a fish'''.}}
# [[of]]; {{n-g|marks possession}}
#: {{ux|tl|kuwento '''ng''' bayan|story '''of''' the town|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|tl|luto '''ng''' kusinero|cooking '''of''' the chef|inline=1}}
# [[to]], [[into]], [[towards]]; in the direction of; arriving at
#: {{ux|tl|Pumasok '''ng''' silid si Tatay nang may dumating na bisita.|Dad went '''into''' his room when visitors had dropped by.}}
#: {{ux|tl|Bibiyahe '''ng''' Saudi ang kaibigan ko para magtrabaho.|My friend will depart '''for''' (to) [[Saudi Arabia|Saudi]] to work.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* See usage notes at {{m|tl|nang}}.

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alt|tl|nang||archaic}}
* {{alt|tl|n͠g|ng̃|ñg||archaic|Abecedario}}

=====See also=====
{{tl-markers}}

===Etymology 2===
From the usage of the {{angbr|ng̃}} digraph in the Spanish-based orthography to represent {{IPAchar|/ŋ/}}.
Each pronunciation has a different source:
* Filipino alphabet pronunciation is influenced by {{der|tl|en|n}} and {{der|tl|en|g}}.
* Abakada alphabet pronunciation is influenced by Baybayin character {{m|tl|{{tl-bay sc|nga}}}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-IPA|én dyi|pre={{sense|letter name|Filipino alphabet}}}}
{{tl-IPA|nga|pre={{sense|letter name|Abakada alphabet}}}}
{{tl-IPA|ŋ|pre={{sense|phoneme}}}}
* {{rhymes|tl|en dji|a|s1=2|s2=1}}

====Letter====
{{head|tl|letters|lower case||upper case|Ng|Baybayin spelling|{{tl-bay sc|en dyi}}|f3sc=Tglg|cat2=terms with Baybayin script}}

# {{Latn-def-lite|tl|letter|16|en dyi|alphabet name={{w|Filipino alphabet}}}}

=====See also=====
* {{list:Latin script letters/tl}}
* {{l|tl|g̃}}

====Letter====
{{head|tl|letters|lower case||upper case|Ng|Baybayin spelling|{{tl-bay sc|nga}}|f3sc=Tglg|cat2=terms with Baybayin script}}

# {{Latn-def-lite|tl|letter|12|nga|alphabet name={{w|Abakada alphabet}}}}

===Etymology 3===

====Adverb====
{{tl-adv|b=endyi}}

# {{alt form|tl|n.g.}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:Pambansang Diksiyonaryo}}

-------- end text --------
