Page 17125 Sangley: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|sangley}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|es|Sangley}}, ultimately from {{der|tl|nan-hbl|-}}, possibly from:
* {{m|nan-hbl|[[常]][[來]]|tr=siâng lâi|lit=frequently comes}}, as labeled in the {{w|Boxer Codex}} (1590), from which {{cog|cmn|常來人|tr=chángláirén}} referring to Sangleys originates and consistent with {{w|Francisco de Sande}} (1576)'s description of {{m-g|''Throughout these islands they call the Chinese 'Sangleyes', meaning 'a  people who come and go,' on account of their habit of coming annually to these islands to trade, or, as they say there, 'the regular port'.''}} as per Manuel (1948)<ref name="Manuel" />
* {{m|nan-hbl|生理|tr=seng-lí|pos=[[IPA]]: {{IPAchar|/ɕiɪŋ³³ li⁵⁵⁴/}}|gloss=business; livelihood}} as in {{m|nan-hbl|生理人|tr=seng-lí-lâng|t=merchant; tradesman; trafficker}}, according to Go (2014-2015)<ref name="Go" /> and  Manuel (1948)<ref name="Manuel" /> as {{m-g|''sieng-lí}} recorded in Piñol (1937)
* {{m|nan-hbl|商旅|tr=siang-lí|gloss=travelling merchant}}, according to Manuel (1948)<ref name="Manuel" /> & Hofileña (2011),<ref name="Hofileña" /> which Go (2014-2015) considers to be "a rather literal term uncommon among early Chinese in the Philippines".
* {{m|nan-hbl|[[送]][[來]]|tr=sàng lâi|lit=sent over}} or {{m|nan-hbl|[[生理人]][[來]]|tr=seng-lí-lâng lâi|lit=businessman comes}}, according to Chan-Yap (1980). <ref name="Chan-Yap />
* {{m|nan-hbl|[[誰]][[來]]|tr=siâng lâi / sâng lâi|lit=who comes}}, as per Norberto Romualdez in Manuel (1948) who says that {{m-g|''sangley is a Spanish corruption of '''sang lay''', signifying "who comes", an expression used by the Chinese in the Islands to greet a ship coming from China.''}}<ref name="Manuel" />
{{doublet|tl|Sanglay}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|Sangléy}}

===Noun===
