Page 18231 sungsong: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|songsong||obsolete|Abecedario orthography}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|tl|poz-pro|*suŋsuŋ||go against wind or current}}.<ref name="Blust"/> Compare {{cog|pag|songsong||contradict; go against the tide}}, {{cog|pam|tapayan sungsung||Chinese jar}}, {{cog|agn|tongtong||go against the current}}, {{cog|ceb|salungsung||go directly against the current}}. {{cog|tsg|Sungsung||China}}, {{cog|ms|songsong||opposite direction}}, and {{cog|jv|ꦤꦸꦁꦱꦸꦁ||go upstream, toward the source}}. See also {{m|tl|Sungsong}}. According to Blust and Trussel (2010), the attribution to China is presumably connected with sailing problems in reaching mainland China from the Philippines.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|sungsóng|hmp=Sungsong}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|sungsóng|b=+}}

# [[act]] of [[going]] [[against]] the [[current]] or [[wind]] {{gl|such as when sailing}}
#: {{syn|tl|salunga|pagsalunga|salangsang|sugod}}
# {{lb|tl|archaic}} [[north]] of the [[monsoon]]

====Usage notes====
* According to Panganiban (1972),<ref name="Panganiban"/> there is an old tradition that insinuates that {{m|tl|Lusong}} (or {{m|tl|Luzon}}) would be "south of the monsoon" from the Chinese perspective with {{m|tl|Sungsong}} as "north of the monsoon".

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|pagsungsong|manungsong|ipanungsong|sungsungin|isungsong|sumungsong|magsungsong|ipagsungsong}}

====Related terms====
{{rel3|tl|salungsong|salangsang}}

====See also====
{{col3|tl|lusong}}

===Adjective===
{{tl-adj|sungsóng|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|archaic}} [[Chinese]]; of [[Chinese]] [[origin]]
#: {{syn|tl|Tsino|Intsik}}

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|sinamomong-sungsong|suhang-sungsong|sampagitang-sungsong|kabuteng-sungsong|alang-ilang sonson|malagkit sungsong|kamariang-sungsong}}

====See also====
* {{l|tl|Sungsong}}

===References===
<references>
<ref name="Blust">{{R:map:ACD|suŋsuŋ₁}}</ref>
<ref name="Panganiban">{{R:Diksyunaryo tesauro Pilipino-Ingles 1973|page=924}}</ref>
</references>

